---
import SystemPage from '../../components/v2/SystemPage.astro';
import { imagery } from '../../content/imagery';

const v2 = imagery.v2;
---

<SystemPage
	title="Chimney flashing and repair"
	heroEyebrow="Residential · service"
	heroHeading="Chimney flashing.<br />Where most leaks start."
	heroLede="Step flashing, counter flashing, cricket installation, and full chimney rebuilds. The single most common source of residential roof leaks in East Texas — and the most often installed wrong."
	heroPhoto={v2.pageHeros.chimneyFlashing}
	heroCta={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
	bleedPhoto={v2.bleeds.workmanship}
	bleedHeading="Step into the courses.<br />Counter into the brick."
	overview={{
		eyebrow: 'Why chimneys leak',
		heading: 'Two flashings, both have to work.',
		body: "Chimney flashing is a two-piece system: step flashing woven into each shingle course as the roof goes up, and counter flashing cut into the brick or stone above to cover the step flashing. If either is missing, wrong, or aging, water tracks in. Most leaks we see come from chimneys built when the roofer skipped the counter flashing and substituted caulk — which works for about 5 years."
	}}
	keyPoints={[
		{
			heading: 'Step + counter flashing (the right way).',
			body: "Step flashing is interleaved with shingle courses on both sides of the chimney — each piece bent 90 degrees, one leg under the shingle, one leg up the chimney. Counter flashing is set into a cut in the brick mortar (or stone joints) and folded down over the step flashing. The two pieces work together; neither alone is enough.",
			bullets: ['Step flashing woven into shingle courses', 'Counter flashing cut into the brick', 'Mortar reglet or surface-mount termination', 'Sealant only as a finishing detail'],
			photo: v2.serviceTiles.chimney,
			photoSide: 'left',
		},
		{
			heading: 'Crickets — when and why.',
			body: "A cricket (saddle) is a small triangular roof structure built behind the chimney to divert water around it. Code requires a cricket on chimneys 30+ inches wide. Without one, water and debris pile up behind the chimney and back up under the shingles. We install crickets on any chimney that needs one — about half of the chimney repairs we do.",
			bullets: ['Required: chimneys 30"+ wide', 'Diverts water around chimney', 'Prevents debris/ice dam buildup'],
			photo: v2.bleeds.workmanship,
			photoSide: 'right',
		},
	]}
	faq={[
		{ q: "Can you fix a leak without rebuilding the flashing?", a: '<p>Sometimes — if the failure is a single piece of flashing or a cracked sealant joint, a targeted repair holds. If multiple pieces have failed or the original install was wrong (caulk-and-pray), the right fix is a flashing rebuild. We tell you which it is on the inspection.</p>' },
		{ q: "Can you flash a chimney without disturbing the brick?", a: '<p>Surface-mount counter flashing avoids cutting into the brick — we install it on the brick face with stainless fasteners and sealant. Cleaner-looking than mortar-cut on some chimneys; not as durable on others. We pick the method that fits the chimney and the homeowner\'s priorities.</p>' },
		{ q: "Do I need a cricket?", a: '<p>If the chimney is 30+ inches wide measured along the slope, code requires one. Even on smaller chimneys, a cricket is worth installing — the cost is modest and the leak prevention is significant.</p>' },
		{ q: "Why does my chimney leak only on one side?", a: '<p>Water generally enters at the up-slope or one of the side flashings — that\'s where the failed piece is. Most leak diagnostics start on the up-slope (highest-water-flow) side and work down.</p>' },
	]}
	ctaHeading="Chimney leaking?<br />We diagnose to the source."
	ctaLede="Free inspection. Most chimney leaks have a known fix."
	ctaPhoto={v2.pageHeros.freeInspection}
	ctaPrimary={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
/>
